\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Spatial link fields and their transfer kernel}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let #{G} be a compact Lie group, including finite and
disconnected groups, with
[normalized Haar measure](fdip-007B). Fix a
\vocabk{finite incidence presentation}{fdip-0002} with vertex set
#{V_s}, oriented edge set #{E_s} and source and target maps
#{s,t:E_s\to V_s}. The spatial configuration space is
#{X=G^{E_s}}, with product Haar probability #{dU}. Let
#{A(X)} be the algebra of bounded Borel functions
#{X\to\mathbb C} under the pointwise product. The
[gauge action](fdip-006E) of #{K=G^{V_s}} is
##{(h\cdot U)_e=h_{t(e)}U_eh_{s(e)}^{-1},\qquad
 (R_hf)(U)=f(h^{-1}\cdot U).}
Each #{R_h} is an automorphism of #{A(X)} and preserves the
supremum norm.}

\p{All irreducible representations here are complex and unitary.
For a finite set #{F} of them and coefficients #{a_\rho>0}, the
truncation
##{k_F(g)=\sum_{\rho\in F}\dim\rho a_\rho\chi_\rho(g)}
is a finite sum. Write
##{\kappa_F=\sum_{\rho\in F}(\dim\rho)^2a_\rho.}
Unitarity gives #{|\chi_\rho(g)|\le \dim\rho}, so
#{|k_F(g)|\le\kappa_F}, with equality at the identity. For finite
sets #{F\subset F'} carrying the same coefficients,
##{|k_{F'}(g)-k_F(g)|
 \le\kappa_{F'}-\kappa_F
 =\sum_{\rho\in F'\setminus F}(\dim\rho)^2a_\rho,}
with equality at the identity. If #{F} is closed under dual
representations and #{a_{\rho^*}=a_\rho}, then #{k_F} is real,
central and inversion-symmetric. Coefficient positivity does not
imply #{k_F(g)>0}. The scalar #{a_\rho} is the convolution
eigenvalue of this finite sum on the #{\rho} coefficient space;
the character coefficient is #{\dim\rho a_\rho}. If the trivial
representation lies in #{F}, then
#{\int_G k_F\,\extd\mu_G=a_{\mathrm{triv}}}.}

\p{Let #{V:X\to\mathbb R} be bounded Borel and pointwise
gauge invariant, and set #{b(U)=\ue^{-V(U)/2}}. Then #{b} and
#{b^{-1}} are bounded Borel functions. If #{M_b} denotes
multiplication by #{b}, define
##{\begin{aligned}
c_F(U,U')&=\prod_{e\in E_s}k_F(U_eU_e'^{-1}),\\
(C_{k_F}f)(U)&=\int_Xc_F(U,U')f(U')\,dU',\\
T_F&=M_bC_{k_F}M_b.
\end{aligned}}
For #{f\in A(X)} the integral is a bounded Borel function, since
the kernel is a finite sum of continuous characters and
#{|c_F|\le\kappa_F^{|E_s|}}. This is the truncated transfer
operator for the specified finite data. Its integral kernel is
#{b(U)c_F(U,U')b(U')}. If #{V} is continuous, the kernel is
continuous. The spatial potential may be a sum of Wilson
plaquette terms; boundedness, Borel measurability and the stated
invariance suffice.}

\p{The factorization separates the two spatial half-weights from
the temporal convolution. It parallels the gauge factors in
\citet{Eqs. (17), (20)–(21), p. 287}{luscher1977transfer};
the [source comparison](fdip-0087) distinguishes this pure-gauge
model from that paper's gauge and fermion construction.}

\p{Remark. Continuum comparison. The convergent series and the
#{L^2} setting are not the definition above. For a real
continuous central kernel satisfying the
\vocabk{positive coefficient conditions}{fdip-007I},
##{k(g)=\sum_{\rho\in\widehat G}\dim\rho a_\rho\chi_\rho(g),
 \qquad a_\rho>0,\qquad
 \sum_{\rho\in\widehat G}(\dim\rho)^2a_\rho<\infty,}
the expansion is uniformly absolutely convergent. The
\newvocab{link Hilbert space} is
##{\mathcal H_{\mathrm{link}}=L^2(X,dU;\mathbb C),}
on which every #{R_h} is unitary. The operator #{T=M_bC_kM_b}
defined by the same formulas with this kernel #{k} is the
\newvocab{pure-gauge transfer operator} on that space. Its
integral kernel is bounded Borel and bounded below by a positive
constant when #{k(g)>0}. If #{k} is a probability density, then
#{a_{\mathrm{triv}}=\int_G k\,\extd\mu_G=1}. The
[heat kernel](fdip-00ML) supplies this comparison, for every
compact connected Lie group, through the series recorded in its
continuum comparison. Each finite set #{F} recovers the
truncation #{k_F} displayed above.}
